From e051996ac435dd7719601cc1caab1e9c817e0abf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 04:54:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] fix(#45): a memory cap the OS refuses (macOS) is held by
 a resident-memory watchdog, and the page names the bound in force

Defect, verbatim: "a --mem-limit-mb cap the OS refuses (macOS) is never reported, and Linux interception is
unmeasured". Linux interception now works (memory_cut); where setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS) is refused there was no
bound at all, the run reported memory_standing telemetry_only, and #39's own shape (take over an infinite
generator, list(islice(seq, n)) mutated to unbounded) grew until something outside Wesker stopped it.

The obvious fix (poll maxrss) is wrong: ru_maxrss is a lifetime peak and never falls, so a later mutant
would read as over the cap for an earlier one's spike. The watchdog reads CURRENT resident memory and
judges GROWTH since the window opened, like memory_budget_standing.

Pure decisions (pinned):
  memory_bound_standing(cap_requested, rlimit_applied, rss_readable)
    rlimit    the OS accepted the cap. Primary: it fails the allocation itself, and resident memory cannot
              exceed the address space that holds it, so a watchdog beside it could only fire second. Not armed.
    watchdog  refused, but current resident memory is readable: a poller stops the mutant.
    none      no cap asked, or nothing can enforce one.
  watchdog_standing(rss, baseline, limit, last_fire)
    unreadable / within / fire / held. After a stop a further one needs growth of an eighth of the cap
    beyond the size at the last stop (a mutant that swallowed the stop and went on allocating); pages the
    allocator kept after the mutant unwound are held, not a runaway, since a second injection would land in
    the harness.
  merge_memory_bound(current, observed)  the stronger bound of a function's workers.
  memory_rollup_bound(bounds)  not_recorded / rlimit / watchdog / none / mixed; n/a says nothing.

MemoryWatchdog raises MemoryError in the thread running the mutant (the worker's main thread; for the
in-process path the JoinedWorker the measuring thread started after the window opened, never the measuring
thread), so the node fails exactly as under RLIMIT_AS and memory_cut_standing / memory_cut_run_standing read
it unchanged: the same unscored memory_cut, never a kill. On exit it joins its thread and clears any stop
still pending, so none lands in the harness. memory_standing keeps its vocabulary (enforced now means a bound
held); the new memory_bound field says which, in ProfilingResult, the rollup and shards, and
describe_memory. An in-process run is no longer described as "NOT bounded" when RLIMIT_AS or the watchdog
bounds it under the run's budget.

Not closed: the watchdog acts at the next bytecode, so a single allocation that never returns to the
interpreter is stopped only when it does (RLIMIT_AS, where the OS accepts it, is the bound for that); a
mutant that swallows every stop in a loop is re-stopped only after further growth, so the wall clock is
still its last bound. Real macOS is not verified (simulated on Linux by making setrlimit raise, as the OS
does); the macOS resident-size route is the existing mach_task_basic_info call. Detective still passes no
cap (separate item). Policy id unchanged (tests/test_policy.py green).

Receipt: pins attempted after commit (see report).
tests/test_memory_watchdog_intent.py 55 passed (3.14 and 3.12); neighbouring memory/guard files 277 passed
on both; with the watchdog disarmed 5 of 55 fail, with the pending-stop clear removed 1 fails.
ruff clean under pinned 0.16.7
