+      "rlimit"    the OS accepted ``RLIMIT_AS``. It is the primary bound because it fails the
+                  allocation ITSELF, so even one huge C allocation cannot pass it. It is also never
+                  weaker than the watchdog: resident memory cannot exceed the address space that
+                  holds it, so a watchdog armed beside it could only fire second. Not armed.
+      "watchdog"  the OS refused (or does not offer) the limit but this platform can read the
+                  process's CURRENT resident memory: a poller stops the mutant when it grows past the
+                  cap. Weaker than ``rlimit`` in exactly one way (it acts at the next bytecode, so a
+                  single allocation that never returns to the interpreter is not interrupted); better
+                  than nothing in every other way. This is macOS's answer.
+      "none"      no cap was asked for, or nothing can enforce one: memory is observed, not bounded.
